The diff shows a change in a replaceAll regex: originally replaceAll(""([
]+)"", ""$1# ""), now replaceAll(""([
])"", ""$1# ""). This removes the '+' quantifier, meaning only single newline or carriage return replaced, not sequences. This likely addresses blank lines being not commented out properly: previously multiple newline characters (blank lines) would be matched as a group, then replacement would produce ""# "" after the group, but maybe blank lines become not commented? Actually original: replaceAll ""([
]+)"" with ""$1# "" means for a sequence of newline characters, it captures them as one group then adds ""# "". For blank lines (i.e., consecutive newline characters), the replacement would result in only one ""# "" after the first newline, leaving blank lines un-commented? Changing to ""([
])"" matches each newline individually, ensuring each line gets ""# "" after newline, commenting out blank lines. So this is definitely a bug fix.

